@page "/help"
@layout BareLayout
@inject ReportStore Store

@* The user guide, opened in its own window from the help button in the header (as in GS.Point3DWeb). In-page
   links use "help#…" (not "#…"): a bare fragment would resolve against the base address and go to the Run tab. *@

<PageTitle>Help · GS Test Harness</PageTitle>

<div class="help-page">
    <header class="help-header">
        <span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h1>Help</h1>
        <span class="help-version">GS Test Harness v@(typeof(Program).Assembly.GetName().Version?.ToString(3))</span>
    </header>

    <div class="help-body">
        <nav class="help-toc" aria-label="Contents">
            <ol>
                <li><a href="help#start">Getting started</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#servers">The server list</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#run">The Run tab</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#sites">Sites</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#options">What the run may do</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#results">Reading the results</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#reports">The Reports tab</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#compare">The Compare tab</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#recommendations">The Recommendations tab</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#checks">What gets checked</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#source">Where to fix</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#cli">From the command line</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#files">Where things are stored</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#trouble">Troubleshooting</a></li>
            </ol>
        </nav>

        <article class="help-text">
            <p class="lead">
                GS Test Harness checks a telescope server for ASCOM compliance: <strong>GSServer</strong> through its COM
                driver, or <strong>GreenSwampAlpaca</strong> over Alpaca. It calls every ITelescopeV4 member (and, for
                Alpaca, the HTTP protocol), and for each problem says what's wrong, what ASCOM expects, how to fix it and
                where the code to change probably is.
            </p>

            <section id="start">
                <h2>Getting started</h2>
                <ol>
                    <li>Add the server to the <strong>Servers</strong> list on the left (see <a href="help#servers">The server list</a>).</li>
                    <li>Drag it onto the <strong>Server and sites</strong> card on the <strong>Run</strong> tab (or select it and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>).</li>
                    <li>Tick the sites to run at, choose what the run may do, and press <strong>Start run</strong> beside the server.
                        A warning asks you to remove the OTAs and watch the mount for the whole run; clicking <strong>Start</strong>
                        there accepts responsibility for the run.</li>
                    <li>Read the findings as they come in. Each run is saved; open it later on the <strong>Reports</strong> tab.</li>
                </ol>
                <p class="warning">Moving the mount is off by default. Turn it on only for a simulator, or a real mount that's clear to move.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="servers">
                <h2>The server list</h2>
                <p>The buttons above the list:</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Add (+)</dt>
                    <dd>A new entry: a name, which server it is, and its Alpaca host, port and device number (with a user
                        name and password for a server that asks for one), or the COM driver's ProgID.</dd>
                    <dt>Alpaca Discovery (magnifier)</dt>
                    <dd>Searches the local network for Alpaca telescopes and lists the ones that answer. Pick one to add it.</dd>
                    <dt>ASCOM Drivers (telescope)</dt>
                    <dd>Lists the ASCOM telescope drivers installed on this computer. Windows only.</dd>
                    <dt>Connect (plug)</dt>
                    <dd>Connects to, or disconnects from, the selected server, to see that it answers. A run connects by itself.</dd>
                    <dt>Save (disk)</dt>
                    <dd>Saves the list now. It's also saved after every change.</dd>
                    <dt>Delete (bin)</dt>
                    <dd>Removes the selected server from the list. Its reports are kept.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p>The dot before a server is green while it's connected. Double-click a server (or press <kbd>F2</kbd>)
                    to edit it, and press <kbd>Delete</kbd> to remove it. The <strong>«</strong> button folds the list
                    into a narrow strip; this browser remembers the choice.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="run">
                <h2>The Run tab</h2>
                <p>The <strong>Server and sites</strong> card holds the server to test. Drag one onto it from the list;
                    <strong>✕</strong> clears it. It also shows where the server's source code was found, so findings can
                    name a file (see <a href="help#source">Where to fix</a>).</p>
                <p><strong>Start run</strong> sits beside the server. With more than one site ticked it reads
                    <strong>Start 2 runs</strong> or <strong>Start 3 runs</strong>: one run, and one report, per site.
                    While it runs, a bar shows the step and progress, with <strong>Stop</strong> to end it early (the mount
                    is still put back). The findings appear below as they come in.</p>
                <p>When it's done, the bar links to each report and, for several sites, to <strong>Compare sites</strong>.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="sites">
                <h2>Sites</h2>
                <p>A run can be made at up to three sites, ticked under the server:</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Server's current settings</dt>
                    <dd>Whatever the server is set to now. Nothing is changed.</dd>
                    <dt>Northern Hemisphere and Southern Hemisphere</dt>
                    <dd>A name, latitude, longitude (east positive) and elevation in metres. A run there writes
                        SiteElevation, SiteLatitude and SiteLongitude after connecting, and puts the server's own values
                        back before disconnecting, also if the run fails or is stopped.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p>A northern site needs a latitude above 0, a southern one below 0. A site with a mistake can't be ticked.
                    The sites and ticks are shared by every browser tab.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="options">
                <h2>What the run may do</h2>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Move the mount</dt>
                    <dd>Slews, syncs, MoveAxis, PulseGuide, park and find home. Tracking and park state are put back afterwards.</dd>
                    <dt>Write settings back unchanged and try invalid values</dt>
                    <dd>Site, guide rates, tracking rate and settle time. Anything wrongly accepted is put back.</dd>
                    <dt>Call SetPark</dt>
                    <dd>Moves the saved park position to wherever the mount is pointing. Needs <em>Move the mount</em>.</dd>
                    <dt>Alpaca protocol checks</dt>
                    <dd>GreenSwampAlpaca only: management API, transaction IDs, error replies, HTTP 400 and discovery.</dd>
                    <dt>Extended checks</dt>
                    <dd>Adds a few minutes: tracking drift (on, off, rate offsets), several clients at once, parallel reads and connect cycles.</dd>
                    <dt>Disconnect at the end</dt>
                    <dd>Checks NotConnected errors. Only possible when no other client holds the device.</dd>
                    <dt>Slew timeout and call timeout</dt>
                    <dd>How long a slew, and any single call, may take before it counts as never answered.</dd>
                    <dt>Flip angle</dt>
                    <dd>Degrees either side of the meridian (0 to 90) where the mount can point from either side of the pier.
                        Inside it the flip checks flip the mount with the SideOfPier command, halfway in on each side, and it must
                        keep pointing at the same place. A goto 5° past it, across the meridian, must flip the mount by itself.
                        Needs <em>Move the mount</em>.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>

            <section id="results">
                <h2>Reading the results</h2>
                <p>Each finding has an outcome:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge fail">Fail</span></td><td>Breaks the ASCOM rules. Fix it.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge error">Error</span></td><td>The call threw something unexpected, or never answered.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge warning">Warning</span></td><td>Allowed, but likely to trouble some clients.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge pass">Pass</span></td><td>As ASCOM expects.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge info">Info</span></td><td>Worth knowing; not a problem.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge">Skipped</span></td><td>Not checked: not supported, or not allowed by the run's options.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>The counts above the list are filters: click one to show that outcome, click more to add theirs, and click one again to take it away; with none picked, every outcome shows.
                    Narrow it further by group or with the search box. Click a finding to open it: the check's ID, what was
                    expected, what came back, <strong>how to fix</strong> it, <strong>where</strong> in the code, and a
                    reference to the ASCOM rule.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="reports">
                <h2>The Reports tab</h2>
                <p>Every finished run, newest first: when, the device and its version, the site, COM or Alpaca, and a bar
                    of fail, warning and pass. Click the device to open the report. The buttons at the end of a row
                    download it as Markdown (ready to paste into an issue) or JSON.</p>
                <p>To delete reports, tick them (or tick <strong>Select all</strong>) and press <strong>Delete checked</strong>.
                    You're asked first; deleted reports can't be brought back.</p>
                <p>A report opens with its totals and duration, the device's identity, the run's options, and the findings.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="compare">
                <h2>The Compare tab</h2>
                <p>Shows how the server on the Run tab does at each ticked site, side by side: its latest run at each,
                    one column per site. Change the server or the ticks on the Run tab and it follows.</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Differs</dt>
                    <dd>Another outcome, or another message, at some site: usually something that depends on the latitude
                        or longitude, such as pier side, alt/az or sidereal time.</dd>
                    <dt>Fails at every site</dt>
                    <dd>The same problem everywhere.</dd>
                    <dt>Not checked everywhere</dt>
                    <dd>The same result wherever it was made, but some site's run didn't make the check.</dd>
                    <dt>Same, no problem</dt>
                    <dd>Passed everywhere. Hidden until you click its count.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p>Runs at two or more of the ticked sites are needed.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="recommendations">
                <h2>The Recommendations tab</h2>
                <p>Every check that found a problem, across the saved runs, with the recommended fix and where the code
                    probably is. Only the latest run of each device at each site counts, so a problem fixed since an
                    older run doesn't come back. Each server is listed apart, because the fix is in its own code.</p>
                <p>A check is listed once per server, with the worst result first. Under it: the fix (more than one when
                    runs gave different advice), the source locations, the ASCOM reference and each run that found it,
                    with the expected and actual values. A check with no recorded fix shows those values instead.</p>
                <p>Click a count to show failures, errors or warnings (click several to show more than one, and again to take one away); <strong>No problem</strong> also lists the
                    checks that passed. <strong>Download Markdown</strong> saves the list, ready to paste into an issue.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="checks">
                <h2>What gets checked</h2>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Checks</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var g in TestRunner.Groups)
                        {
                            <tr><td><strong>@g.Name</strong></td><td>@g.Description</td></tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <section id="source">
                <h2>Where to fix</h2>
                <p>A finding names the file and line to look at, such as <code>GS.Ascom.SkyTelescope/Telescope.cs:1265</code>,
                    when the server's source code is on this computer. <code>appsettings.json</code> → <code>Codebases</code>
                    says where each server's source is. Folder names are looked for beside this program's folder and
                    its parents, so <code>GSServer</code> and <code>GreenSwampAlpaca</code> cloned next to it are found
                    by themselves. The Server and sites card shows the folder found, or says none was.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="cli">
                <h2>From the command line</h2>
                <p>For scripts and build pipelines, <code>--run</code> checks one device, saves the report like a run from
                    this page, and exits:</p>
                <pre><code>GS.TestHarness --run GreenSwampAlpaca --host localhost --port 31416 --device 0 [--motion] [--md report.md]
GS.TestHarness --run GSServer [--progid ASCOM.GS.Sky.Telescope]</code></pre>
                <p>Other options: <code>--setpark</code>, <code>--no-writes</code>, <code>--no-protocol</code>,
                    <code>--no-disconnect</code>, <code>--extended</code>, <code>--drift-seconds &lt;s&gt;</code>,
                    <code>--sites server,north,south</code> (the North and South saved here), <code>--slew-timeout &lt;s&gt;</code>
                    and <code>--call-timeout &lt;s&gt;</code>.</p>
                <p>The exit code is 0 when nothing failed, 1 when there were failures or errors, and 2 for bad arguments.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="files">
                <h2>Where things are stored</h2>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Reports</dt>
                    <dd><code>@Store.Folder</code>, one JSON file per run (setting <code>ReportsFolder</code>).</dd>
                    <dt>Servers and sites</dt>
                    <dd><code>servers.json</code> and <code>sites.json</code> beside the reports folder. Alpaca passwords
                        are saved encrypted (with DPAPI on Windows) and never written into reports.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>

            <section id="trouble">
                <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Alpaca Discovery finds nothing</dt>
                    <dd>The server must be on the same network, with discovery on and UDP port 32227 not blocked by a
                        firewall. Otherwise add it with + and its host and port.</dd>
                    <dt>The ASCOM Drivers button is missing</dt>
                    <dd>It's only there on Windows. A COM driver also needs the ASCOM Platform installed.</dd>
                    <dt>The Disconnected checks are skipped</dt>
                    <dd>Another client (a planetarium, a guider, GS Point3D Web) is holding the device. Close it, or
                        untick <em>Disconnect at the end</em>.</dd>
                    <dt>Slews time out</dt>
                    <dd>Raise the slew timeout, or check that the mount (or simulator) is unparked and can reach the targets.</dd>
                    <dt>Findings don't name a file</dt>
                    <dd>The server's source wasn't found: see <a href="help#source">Where to fix</a>.</dd>
                    <dt>Start run is greyed out</dt>
                    <dd>Tick at least one site under the server.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>
        </article>
    </div>
</div>
